Research software engineers (RSEs) increasingly build scientific tools that depend on commercial large language model (LLM) APIs, where reproducibility and grounding constraints diverge from industrial product engineering. This paper describes the building experience of SDOHPlace Data Discovery Platform, an LLM-augmented public health data discovery application, as an RSE on a single grant-funded project. This paper names six build patterns that the RSE team encountered for LLM gateway development and reports a 30-query ablation comparing three ontology-integration strategies under a model swap. Deterministic ontology matching yielded identical results across ontology-swap conditions for 57% of queries. The remaining 13 of 30 queries fell through to the language model and produced divergent outputs, mirroring the behavior observed in the prompt-side and baseline configurations. These findings suggest that the reproducibility manifest represents an under-recognized RSE infrastructure need, one that the RSE community is particularly well positioned to standardize.